llama.cpp (as fetched by Ollama v0.35.1, llama.cpp b11232, applied after
113): at one sequence, GATED_DELTA_NET reads its recurrent state from the
cache row instead of from a GET_ROWS copy of it.
Generic (all GPUs): not gfx803-specific code; measured on gfx803.

WHY
---
Single-token decode on an RX 590 pays for every dispatch as well as for the
work in it: each one costs about 4.3 us of command-processor time however
small the kernel, a 2.65 us floor plus 1.65 us for the vector-L1 invalidate
that every dispatch needs on gfx8 (docs/FINDINGS.md, "A kernel can read
stale data from the vector L1 across dispatches"). A Qwen3.6 35B-A3B decode
token with the routed experts in system RAM issues about 1,425 dispatches,
about 1,064 of them on the critical path.

Each Gated DeltaNet layer (Qwen3.5/3.6, Qwen3-Next) starts a step by
copying its recurrent state out of the recurrent cache with GET_ROWS, one
2 MiB row on Qwen3.6 35B-A3B (32 heads of 128 x 128 f32), and
GATED_DELTA_NET reads that copy. The op's fused snapshot store already
writes the new state straight into the cache row. For one sequence the copy
only moves bytes the op can read where they are: 30 dispatches and 60 MiB
of copies per token on Qwen3.6 35B-A3B (30 DeltaNet layers).

Alone, with HIP graphs off (tg128 x5, two rounds): 26.30 -> 26.93 tok/s (+2.4%).

Measured on an RX 590 (llama-bench, Qwen3.6 35B-A3B Q4_K_M, all experts in
system RAM with -ncmoe 99, tg128, 5 repetitions, page cache dropped before
each run, HSA_ENABLE_SDMA=1 ROC_CPU_WAIT_FOR_SIGNAL=1), patches 113-116
together: 25.60 -> 27.51 tok/s with HIP graphs (the default), 26.21 ->
27.93 tok/s with GGML_CUDA_DISABLE_GRAPHS=1. With this patch alone and with
all four, greedy 128-token output is byte-identical to the build without
them, and llama-perplexity with -ub 1 (every token through the decode path)
gives mean KLD 0 and the same perplexity in every digit.

WHAT
----
ggml_cuda_try_defer_state_rows runs before ggml_cuda_try_fuse for every
node. On the main stream, with no concurrent streams active, it skips a
GET_ROWS that copies one f32 row (one row id) out of a cache when its only
user is a RESHAPE read only by a GATED_DELTA_NET as its state, the op is
not KDA, has one sequence and an f32 contiguous state
(ggml_cuda_gated_delta_net_state_from_rows_ok), and no node in between
writes the cache or the row ids. The skipped node is recorded in the
context's deferred_state_rows, cleared at the start of every graph compute.
GATED_DELTA_NET then launches a template variant (state_ids_t) that reads
the state at cache + ids[0] * row stride; like the GET_ROWS, it reads the
row id on the device, so a captured HIP graph needs nothing new. Reading
and storing the same cache row in one kernel is safe because each warp
reads its whole state column before it writes that column, and no warp
writes another warp's column. With several sequences one sequence's store
could hit a row another sequence still has to read, which is why the path
takes one sequence only. graph_optimize keeps the row ids allocated until
the GATED_DELTA_NET runs. GGML_CUDA_DISABLE_FUSION=1 turns this off.

Numerics are unchanged: the kernel is the same code with a different state
pointer; on gfx803 the two variants compile to identical floating-point
instruction sequences.

diff --git a/ggml/src/ggml-cuda/common.cuh b/ggml/src/ggml-cuda/common.cuh
index 362c781..b2ae494 100644
--- a/ggml/src/ggml-cuda/common.cuh
+++ b/ggml/src/ggml-cuda/common.cuh
@@ -1685,6 +1685,26 @@ struct ggml_backend_cuda_context {
             mmvq_src1_invalidate();
         }
     }
+
+    // recurrent-state GET_ROWS that the evaluation loop did not run because the GATED_DELTA_NET reading
+    // them reads the cache row through the row ids itself. Filled while walking one graph, cleared
+    // before the next.
+    struct gdn_state_rows {
+        const ggml_tensor * rows;        // the GET_ROWS node that was not run
+        const float *       cache;       // first byte of row 0 of the cache view it reads
+        const int32_t *     ids;         // its row ids (device)
+        int64_t             row_stride;  // floats between cache rows
+    };
+    std::vector<gdn_state_rows> deferred_state_rows;
+
+    const gdn_state_rows * deferred_state_rows_find(const ggml_tensor * rows) const {
+        for (const gdn_state_rows & r : deferred_state_rows) {
+            if (r.rows == rows) {
+                return &r;
+            }
+        }
+        return nullptr;
+    }
 };
 
